From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ae-automation-bot <ae-automation-bot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 07:14:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 037/202] [ae-patch] Calm the pull request cards: a state icon,
 one Ready line and a divided confirm step

Reid asked on 2026-10-14 for the merge cards to look better designed. His
screenshot showed three outlined chips that read as buttons, a confirm step
that nested a Recommended pill inside a segment inside a bordered box inside
the card, and a title cut to one line.

The header now leads with the pull request's state in GitHub's colors (open
green, merged purple, closed red, draft gray), lets the title wrap to two
lines, and puts the short repo name and number beside it (under it on a
narrow card). The whole header is one link.

A card whose Merge is live, with no conflicts and passing (or no) checks,
shows one line, "Ready to merge" with the check count, in place of the
chips. Otherwise the chips are quiet: an icon and a few words each, a
different icon shape per tone, and a red tint only for failing checks,
conflicts or requested changes.

The confirm step is a section of the card under a divider. "Recommended"
leads the reason line instead of tagging the toggle, and the confirm button
names the method and the base branch ("Squash into main", "Merge into
main"), since the question line that named the base is gone. A merged card
reads Merged in purple and drops the chips, which described the open pull
request.

The icons are Tabler outline icons inlined in aePrIcons.ts; they replace
this card's lucide icons.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
